<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: Late Monday Reading
</h4>

<p>
Oh, Lordy. The anecdotal lead argument again.<br />
Could we move beyond this great fog bank of a topic to some clarity and light?<br />
First: The anecdotal lead is NOT THE PROBLEM. Not at all. Not in any way, shape or form.<br />
The Problem is bad writing.  You do not clarify the problem of bad writing by saying, "oh, it's the anecdotal lead." Or, "it's the inverted pyramid." Or, "it's the long-form  narrative." These are structures, folks, like the sonata form and the still-life and the minuet. They're supposed to be used to further other, deeper aims. Sometimes they're abused. Do we abandon the sonata form because someone once composed a bad one?<br />
For all this jabber about leads, anecdotal and otherwise, it seems to me there's not much talk about these deeper aims. We live by them and die by them, and all the while pretend that they're not there. ("I dunno, I just write...")<br />
So let me stick my neck out:<br />
This is a forum, not just of journalists, but of writing journalists. Let's keep it simple: Of Writers. <br />
I can't think of any essential way to distinguish really fine journalistic writing from really fine writing of any other kind, can you? Addison and Steele couldn't. Daniel Defoe couldn't. Ernie Pyle couldn't. McPhee and Talese probably can't. So why should we? (That journalists write "fact" and not "fiction" doesn't count; it's the same language.)<br />
If we are all Writers here, we all bring the same tools to the job and we all hope for the same effect: Language that goes straight from our fingertips to the hearts and minds of our readers. Because if we do not somehow insinuate our words into those hearts and minds, we are all dead donkeys.<br />
Since writing is a craft, we learn to use our tools in apprenticeship, by watching other writers who are better than we are to see how they use the tools.     <br />
I plunder every writer of every kind for what I can get away with and make my own. I'll bet you do to.<br />
For every bleating sheep who says that he or she scorns the anecdotal lead, I can show you eight dozen writers who have used it brilliantly and never exhausted its possibilities. For every damn fool who declares the inverted pyramid dead, I can find a hundred stories that use the form with the beauty and precision of a sonnet.<br />
If we can't talk about what we do with any more insight and imagination than this, how will we ever be able to DO what we do? <br />
   <br />
   <br />
      <br />

</p>
